Fixes #1990.

## What changed

`readLines()` in `packages/code-interpreter-js/src/utils.ts` appended every
decoded chunk to a single string buffer and re-scanned the whole buffer with
`buffer.indexOf('\n')` (plus `buffer.slice()` copies) on every read. For a
long line with no newlines — a base64-encoded image, a wide DataFrame, large
JSON — that is O(n²) in line length and blocks the Node event loop.

The fix decodes and scans only the newly arrived chunk, accumulates line
fragments in an array, and joins once per emitted line. Streaming
`TextDecoder` usage (multi-byte characters split across chunks still decode
correctly), the trailing partial line yielded at stream end, `\r` preservation
(lines are split on `\n` only), and `reader.releaseLock()` in `finally` are
all unchanged.

Per the repo's "equivalent changes in JS and Python" rule I checked the
Python side: both the sync and async code-interpreter stream lines through
httpx's `iter_lines()` / `aiter_lines()`, whose `LineDecoder` already
accumulates fragments in a list and joins once per emitted line — no
equivalent bug there, so no Python change is needed.

## Reproducer

Save as `repro-1990.mjs` and run with `node repro-1990.mjs` (Node 22+, no
dependencies). It feeds a single-line stream with no newlines through
`readLines()` in 64 KiB chunks — `readLinesBefore` is the implementation on
main before this PR, `readLinesAfter` is this PR's version:

```js
// Reproducer for https://github.com/e2b-dev/E2B/issues/1990
// Run with: node repro-1990.mjs   (Node 22+, no dependencies)
//
// Feeds a single-line (no newlines) byte stream — like a base64-encoded
// image, a wide DataFrame, or large JSON — through readLines() in 64 KiB
// chunks and measures wall time.
//
// `readLinesBefore` is the implementation on main before the fix
// (packages/code-interpreter-js/src/utils.ts @ 62a17df), copied verbatim.
// `readLinesAfter` is the fixed version from this PR.

async function* readLinesBefore(stream) {
  const reader = stream.getReader()
  const decoder = new TextDecoder()
  let buffer = ''

  try {
    while (true) {
      const { done, value } = await reader.read()

      if (value !== undefined) {
        buffer += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true })
      }

      if (done) {
        buffer += decoder.decode()
        if (buffer.length > 0) {
          yield buffer
        }
        break
      }

      let newlineIdx = -1

      do {
        newlineIdx = buffer.indexOf('\n')
        if (newlineIdx !== -1) {
          yield buffer.slice(0, newlineIdx)
          buffer = buffer.slice(newlineIdx + 1)
        }
      } while (newlineIdx !== -1)
    }
  } finally {
    reader.releaseLock()
  }
}

async function* readLinesAfter(stream) {
  const reader = stream.getReader()
  const decoder = new TextDecoder()
  const fragments = []

  try {
    while (true) {
      const { done, value } = await reader.read()

      if (value !== undefined) {
        const text = decoder.decode(value, { stream: true })
        let start = 0
        let newlineIdx = text.indexOf('\n', start)
        while (newlineIdx !== -1) {
          fragments.push(text.slice(start, newlineIdx))
          yield fragments.join('')
          fragments.length = 0
          start = newlineIdx + 1
          newlineIdx = text.indexOf('\n', start)
        }
        if (start < text.length) {
          fragments.push(text.slice(start))
        }
      }

      if (done) {
        const trailing = decoder.decode()
        if (trailing.length > 0) {
          fragments.push(trailing)
        }
        if (fragments.length > 0) {
          yield fragments.join('')
        }
        break
      }
    }
  } finally {
    reader.releaseLock()
  }
}

const CHUNK = 64 * 1024

function makeStream(totalBytes) {
  let remaining = totalBytes
  return new ReadableStream({
    start(controller) {
      while (remaining > 0) {
        const n = Math.min(CHUNK, remaining)
        controller.enqueue(new Uint8Array(n).fill(0x78)) // 'x', no newlines
        remaining -= n
      }
      controller.close()
    },
  })
}

async function bench(label, readLines, mb) {
  const lines = []
  const t0 = performance.now()
  for await (const line of readLines(makeStream(mb * 1024 * 1024))) {
    lines.push(line)
  }
  const ms = performance.now() - t0
  const chars = lines.reduce((a, l) => a + l.length, 0)
  console.log(`${label}: ${mb}MB -> ${lines.length} line(s), ${chars} chars, ${ms.toFixed(0)}ms`)
  if (lines.length !== 1 || chars !== mb * 1024 * 1024) {
    throw new Error('wrong output')
  }
}

for (const mb of [10, 40]) await bench('before', readLinesBefore, mb)
for (const mb of [10, 40]) await bench('after ', readLinesAfter, mb)

```

Output (verbatim, measured on this machine):

```
before: 10MB -> 1 line(s), 10485760 chars, 473ms
before: 40MB -> 1 line(s), 41943040 chars, 8987ms
after : 10MB -> 1 line(s), 10485760 chars, 43ms
after : 40MB -> 1 line(s), 41943040 chars, 111ms
```

4× the input → ~19× the time before the fix (quadratic), ~2.6× after
(linear); the 40MB line goes from ~9s of blocked event loop to ~0.1s.

## Tests

- New regression + edge-case tests in `packages/code-interpreter-js/tests/utils.test.ts`:
  a 1MB single line assembled from 1 KiB chunks yields one intact line;
  `\r` kept when splitting on `\n`; newline straddling a chunk boundary;
  empty stream; leading empty chunks.
- `pnpm vitest run tests/utils.test.ts` → 15/15 pass. (The package's other
  test files are live-API integration tests requiring `E2B_API_KEY` and fail
  identically without the key on unmodified main.)
- `pnpm run format`, `pnpm run lint` (0 errors; 1 pre-existing warning in
  untouched `src/messaging.ts`), `pnpm run typecheck` all pass.

## Changeset

Patch changeset for `@e2b/code-interpreter` included.

